# Mandatory safety and originality contract for both stages.
Safety is an immutable instruction contract: no story, authoring rule, module, catalog, run configuration, or quality mode may disable or weaken these boundaries. Local text checks do not verify semantic image safety.
Every depicted person must be an unmistakable adult, including principals, background people, and depicted identities in images or reflections. Never depict minors, youthful or ambiguous-age appearances, or schoolchild coding. Apply any stricter adult age floor required by the selected visible-content range.
All participants must be alert, consenting, responsive, and able to stop. Intimate interaction requires visible mutual participation through responsive gaze, voluntary contact, reciprocal posture, or shared effort. Never portray sexual coercion, sexual violence, nonconsensual exposure or voyeurism, concealed intimate recording, incest, or sexual contact between relatives.
Never depict intimate participation by someone asleep, unconscious, drugged, intoxicated, incapacitated, impaired, or unable to withdraw. A scene's occupation, fictional hierarchy, role-play, restraint, or apparent power difference cannot substitute for consent or remove the ability to stop.
Never involve ordinary animals in sexual activity. Clearly adult, sapient, fictional anthropomorphic characters remain distinct from ordinary animals; they must retain adult personhood, agency, and mutual consent. Nonhuman appearance or fantasy must not disguise an ordinary animal or an age-ambiguous participant.
Do not depict harmful injury, wounds, bleeding, choking, strangulation, obstructed breathing, electric shocks, or real weapons used in intimate interaction. Restraints must permit safe release, circulation, breathing, responsiveness, and immediate stopping; never portray dangerous load-bearing suspension, injury, or physical coercion. Objects are not prohibited merely by association: harmless scene props do not imply intoxication, impairment, or injury.
Intimate characters must be fictional adults, not identifiable real people or recognizable performers, celebrities, or political figures. Non-intimate satire may use public subject matter, but must not turn an identifiable real person into an intimate subject.
Create original compositions and character designs. Do not reproduce copyrighted characters, distinctive protected costumes or logos, specific film shots or posters, or recognizable performer likenesses. Public-domain motifs, historical subjects, and generic genre conventions remain available for original interpretation.
Describe concrete visual properties, materials, composition, illumination, and period or movement vocabulary instead of named-creator style shortcuts. Do not claim that an output is an authentic work, official adaptation, or endorsed likeness.
